This work presents modified gravity theories characterized by a specific form for the interaction between scalar fields and the two fundamental gravitational forces. These models define how additional degrees of freedom influence the expansion rate, distinguishing themselves through the parametrization ${ ar {\mu}}(H,{{\dot H}})$ in addition to ${ ar {
u}}(H,{{\dot H}})$, which control the evolution of matter-scalar coupling strengths. We highlight a newly developed software package, MOGAD, capable of executing full cosmological simulations to explore the resulting non-linear structure formation within this model class. The theoretical framework relies on expressing ${ ar {\mu}}$ and ${ ar {
u}}$ using $f(R)$-based theories via specific formulas. We utilize an evolved set of scalar initial conditions to validate the code's accuracy against perturbative predictions at linear order. Comparisons of our new MOGAD implementation with existing implementations of popular $f(R)$-EAGL models demonstrate that the simulation outputs are consistent, while also showing equivalence between the simulation results and the approximate analytic relations typically employed. Furthermore, we demonstrate how variations in ${ ar {\mu}}$ and ${
u}$ affect the growth factor, the amplitude of cosmic web formation, the formation of dark matter (DM) halo concentrations, the velocity distribution of structures, and the suppression of structure on small scales. Analysis via a 1D velocity PDF confirms the code reproduces key features such as acoustic peaks prominently
